/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A photon absorbed in the depleted absorption layer creates one primary electron–hole pair, exactly as in a PIN.
   - The new carriers are accelerated too and can ionise again, so one primary pair sets off a cascade.
   - M is the avalanche gain (multiplication factor); IM is the multiplied output current and Ip = RP is the primary photocurrent the same light would give with no gain.
   - This is an empirical fit.
   - Multiplication is random: two primary carriers seldom produce the same number of secondary pairs.
   - F is the excess noise factor, M the gain and k the ionisation-rate ratio.
   - ish² is the mean-square shot-noise current at the APD output in A², q = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and B is the receiver bandwidth in Hz.
   - The receiver also has thermal noise ith² from its load or amplifier, which does not depend on M.
   - Silicon APDs have low k and low excess noise, and they work well at 0.8–0.9 µm.
   - An APD needs a much higher bias than a PIN: tens of volts for InGaAs/InP and up to a few hundred volts for some silicon reach-through APDs, always just below breakdown.
*/
