/* book exam traps: Multiplying only the signal and forgetting the excess noise. Signal power grows as M², shot-noise power as M²F. | Saying a bigger M always helps. Past Mopt the extra excess noise outweighs the extra gain, and SNR falls (in the example, 14.7 dB at M = 20 against 14.9 dB at M = 16). | Confusing the ionisation ratio k with the gain M, or with Boltzmann's constant kB. | Writing the SNR from shot noise only. At moderate gain the amplifier's thermal noise is often the larger term. | Using F = M for every APD. That is the worst case, k = 1; a silicon APD with k = 0.02 at M = 100 has F = 0.02 × 100 + 0.98 × 1.99 = 3.95. */
