/* book Worked example: Q: A PIN receiver has responsivity R = 0.8 A/W, received power P = 1 µW, dark current Id = 1 nA, bandwidth B = 100 MHz and load RL = 1 kΩ at T = 300 K. Find the rms shot noise, the rms thermal noise, the total rms noise and the SNR. Is the receiver shot-noise or thermal-noise limited?
   1. Photocurrent: Ip = RP = 0.8 × 1 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.800 µA; Ip + Id = 0.801 µA.
   2. Shot noise: ish² = 2 × 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.801 × 10⁻⁶ × 10⁸ = 2.57 × 10⁻¹⁷ A²; rms 5.07 nA.
   3. Thermal noise: ith² = 4 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300 × 10⁸/1000 = 1.656 × 10⁻¹⁵ A²; rms 40.7 nA.
   4. Total: σ² = 2.57 × 10⁻¹⁷ + 1.656 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 1.682 × 10⁻¹⁵ A²; σ = 41.0 nA.
   5. SNR = (0.800 × 10⁻⁶)²/1.682 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 6.40 × 10⁻¹³/1.682 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 381; SNR(dB) = 10 log₁₀ 381 = 25.8 dB.
   6. Thermal noise is 1.656 × 10⁻¹⁵/2.57 × 10⁻¹⁷ = 64 times the shot noise, so the receiver is thermal-noise limited. Check with the crossover: Ip = 0.8 µA is far below 2kBT/(qRL) = 51.7 µA.
   Answer: Shot noise 5.07 nA rms, thermal noise 40.7 nA rms, total 41.0 nA rms, SNR = 381 (25.8 dB); the receiver is thermal-noise limited. */
